.TE
.SS Compatibility option details
\f[B]\f[CB]\-M, \-\-mood=NAME\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Without this option, the first line of a script file operand selects the
mood.
A Bash shebang selects \f[CR]bash\f[R], an sh or dash shebang selects
\f[CR]sh\f[R], and a kosh shebang selects \f[CR]kosh\f[R], directly or
through \f[CR]env\f[R].
Any other interpreter, or no shebang, leaves the \f[CR]kosh\f[R] mood.
.PP
\f[CR]\-\-mood\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-dumb\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-posix\f[R], and an
invocation basename such as sh or bash take precedence over the shebang,
and \f[CR]\-\-mood\f[R] also takes precedence over \f[CR]\-\-posix\f[R]
and \f[CR]\-o posix\f[R].
The shebang is not used for \f[CR]\-c\f[R], standard input, or
\f[CR]\-\-lint\f[R] without \f[CR]\-I\f[R].
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]\-L, \-\-init\-moods=LIST\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Moods are separated by commas, or the option is repeated.
Without this option, an interactive or login shell uses init_moods from
the settings, and any other shell uses the selected mood.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]\-I, \-\-enable\-mimicry\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
A script file operand always uses the mood selected by its shebang and
the mood precedence rules.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]\-\-dumb\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
An explicit \f[CR]\-\-tab\-selector\f[R] overrides the plain selector
selected by \f[CR]\-\-dumb\f[R].
.SS Auxiliary option details
\f[B]\f[CB]\-\-format\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Command strings are ignored, and more than one file needs
\f[CR]\-\-apply\f[R].
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]\-\-apply\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
\f[CR]\-\-apply\f[R] requires \f[CR]\-\-lint\f[R],
\f[CR]\-\-format\f[R], or both, and at least one file.
Standard input, \f[CR]\-s\f[R], \f[CR]\-i\f[R], \f[CR]\-c\f[R], and a
lone \f[CR]\-\f[R] are rejected.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]\-\-as\-language\-server\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
File operands, \f[CR]\-c\f[R], \f[CR]\-s\f[R], \f[CR]\-i\f[R],
\f[CR]\-\-lint\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-format\f[R], and \f[CR]\-\-apply\f[R] are
rejected.
.SS Koshka option details
\f[B]\f[CB]\-\-no\-config\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
PATH and the startup files are unchanged.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]\-W, \-WW, \-WWW\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Analysis has strict, lenient, and annoying tiers.
In the \f[CR]kosh\f[R] mood, strict and lenient findings are errors and
annoying findings are warnings.
\f[CR]\-W\f[R] keeps that, \f[CR]\-WW\f[R] demotes lenient errors to
warnings, and \f[CR]\-WWW\f[R] also demotes strict errors.
In the other moods no analysis runs until a level is set, and
\f[CR]\-W\f[R], \f[CR]\-WW\f[R], and \f[CR]\-WWW\f[R] enable the strict,
the lenient, and the annoying tier as warnings, each with the tiers
before it.
.PP
A level set with \f[CR]set\f[R] passes to launched shells through
\f[CR]KOSH_ANALYSIS\f[R].
Runtime diagnostics do not depend on the level, except that the
compatibility moods warn about an unset variable that expands to nothing
only when a level is set.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]\-\-tab\-selector MODE\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
MODE is \f[CR]interactive\f[R], the default, \f[CR]external\f[R], or
\f[CR]plain\f[R].
See Line editing.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]\-\-enable\-koshkit\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The option is off by default and affects only the compatibility moods,
since the \f[CR]kosh\f[R] mood always resolves bundled utilities after
PATH.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]\-\-enable\-extended\-arithmetic\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The \f[CR]kosh\f[R] mood keeps extended arithmetic on.
.SS Debug option details
\f[B]\f[CB]\-\-show\-optimizer\-diagnostics\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
A compatibility mood shows these warnings only under \f[CR]\-WW\f[R].
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]\-N, \-\-show\-all\-exit\-codes\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
\f[CR]set \-N\f[R] and \f[CR]set +N\f[R] toggle reporting of every
command status.
.SS POSIX option details
\f[B]\f[CB]\-s, \-\-stdin\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The input order is described under Invocation.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]\-c COMMAND, \-\-command=COMMAND\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The option may be repeated, and the strings run in order.
A string with a syntax error ends the shell with status 2, one that
analysis rejects ends it with status 1, and the later strings do not
run.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]\-n, \-\-no\-exec\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
A compatibility mood reports only the tiers that \f[CR]\-W\f[R] selects.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]\-o NAME, +o NAME\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
\f[CR]\-o posix\f[R] acts as \f[CR]\-\-posix\f[R].
An unknown option exits with status 2.
In the \f[CR]kosh\f[R] mood, \f[CR]+o nounset\f[R] is ignored and
\f[CR]+o pipefail\f[R] exits with status 2.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]+a, +C, +e, +f, +n, +p, +t, +u, +v, +x\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
+h and +m are accepted and ignored.
The \f[CR]set\f[R] letters \-B, \-E, \-H, \-P, \-b, and \-k are not
command\-line options and exit with status 2.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]\-\-\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The next operand is the script.
.SS Bash option details
\f[B]\f[CB]\-\-norc\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
A login shell, its profiles, and BASH_ENV are unaffected.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]\-O NAME, +O NAME\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
An unknown option exits with status 2, and a read\-only option such as
login_shell is ignored.
In the \f[CR]kosh\f[R] mood, \f[CR]+O failglob\f[R] and
\f[CR]\-O nullglob\f[R] exit with status 2.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]\-p, \-\-privileged\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
ENV, BASH_ENV, and the rc script of an interactive non\-login
\f[CR]kosh\f[R] mood shell are not sourced, KOSHCONF is ignored and
removed, and no settings file is read.
A secure execution, such as a setuid or setgid invocation, always drops
the elevated ids and skips every startup and settings file.
.PP
On Linux, such a shell whose real user is not root also clears its
capability sets, including the ambient set, and aborts if it can still
switch to root.
.SH SHELL SETTINGS
Shell settings can be read and changed through \f[CR]set\f[R],
\f[CR]shopt\f[R], and \f[CR]koshconf\f[R], and are reported by the
\f[CR]\-o\f[R] test, SHELLOPTS, and BASHOPTS.
Each setting has a numeric id and a koshconf name.
Some also have a Bash name or an option letter.
Interactive settings affect the editor, history, or diagnostic
presentation.
Semantic settings can change script behavior.
In the Scope column, \f[CR]i\f[R] means interactive and \f[CR]s\f[R]
means semantic.
.PP
The first component of a koshconf name is its topic.
The \f[CR]mood\f[R] and \f[CR]init_moods\f[R] options select moods,
\f[CR]interpreter\f[R] covers shell behavior, \f[CR]compatibility\f[R]
covers Windows path mapping, \f[CR]editor\f[R] covers the line editor
and holds the \f[CR]editor.history\f[R] and \f[CR]editor.hints\f[R]
groups, \f[CR]optimizer\f[R] covers analysis, and \f[CR]completion\f[R]
and \f[CR]debug\f[R] cover completion and debugging output.
.PP
The name of an option from POSIX \f[CR]set \-o\f[R] starts with posix,
and one from another Bash \f[CR]set \-o\f[R] or \f[CR]shopt\f[R] option
starts with bash, as in posix.interpreter.exit_on_command_failure for
errexit.
Every other option exists only in Koshka.
.PP
An option with a Bash spelling is also accepted as posix.legacy.NAME
when POSIX \f[CR]set \-o\f[R] accepts NAME, and as bash.legacy.NAME
otherwise, as in bash.legacy.extglob.
editor.base_mode has the alias posix.legacy.vi only.
The alias is the same option, and every option operand and kosh.conf
line accepts it.
.PP
Error messages, KOSHCONF, \f[CR]koshconf create\f[R], and
\f[CR]koshconf list\f[R] use only the canonical name, and
\f[CR]koshconf list \-\-all\f[R] adds each alias.
The alias of interactive_comments is bash.legacy.interactive\-comments,
spelled as the \f[CR]set \-o\f[R] option.
.PP
A settings file may set every option except those fixed by the
invocation or the session.
bash.interpreter.login_shell and bash.interpreter.restricted_shell are
read\-only.
bash.interpreter.privileged_mode,
posix.interpreter.parse_without_executing, and
bash.interpreter.exit_after_one_command come from the command line.
posix.interpreter.job_control, bash.editor.history.records_commands,
bash.editor.history.bang_expansion, and bash.interpreter.posixly_correct
follow the session and the mood.
.PP
editor.base_mode covers the Bash options emacs and vi.
It is vi while \f[CR]set \-o vi\f[R] is on and emacs otherwise, and
\f[CR]set \-o emacs\f[R], \f[CR]set \-o vi\f[R], the \f[CR]\-o\f[R]
test, and SHELLOPTS behave as in Bash.
The Bash options hashall and nolog have no koshconf name.
\f[CR]set\f[R] and \f[CR]\-o\f[R] accept them, and
\f[CR]koshconf list \-\-all\f[R] shows them as posix.legacy.hashall and
posix.legacy.nolog.
koshconf get, koshconf set, and the settings files reject these
spellings.
The bash.legacy shopt options in the Bash and POSIX settings tables
change nothing, so Bash startup files that set them run unchanged.
shopt accepts array_expand_once as the Bash 5.3 spelling of
assoc_expand_once.
.SS Koshka options
.PP
.TS
