.TE
.SH CONFIGURATION EXAMPLES
A user file that keeps the Bash grammar and edits with vi keys is
.IP
.EX
mood=bash
editor.history.max_entries=20000
editor.base_mode=vi
.EE
.PP
A user file that keeps the \f[CR]kosh\f[R] mood and sources
\[ti]/.bashrc in the \f[CR]bash\f[R] mood at interactive startup is
.IP
.EX
init_moods=bash
.EE
.PP
\f[CR]koshconf set\f[R] \f[I]option value\f[R] \f[CR]\-\-persist\f[R]
writes the same lines.
.SH SETTINGS FILE LOCATIONS
\f[B]\f[CB]/etc/kosh.conf or %ProgramData%\[rs]kosh\[rs]kosh.conf\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The system settings file, loaded first and only when no other user could
have written it.
.PP
On POSIX, the file and its directory must be owned by root and not
writable by group or others.
.PP
On Windows, the file and its \f[I]kosh\f[R] directory must be owned by
SYSTEM, Administrators, or TrustedInstaller, and no access control entry
may let another account modify them, change their permissions, or take
ownership.
The directory may let others add files.
.PP
A file that fails the check is skipped with a warning.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/kosh/kosh.conf or \[ti]/.config/kosh/kosh.conf\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The user settings file, loaded second.
XDG_CONFIG_HOME applies when it is an absolute path, and on Windows the
directory falls back to USERPROFILE when HOME is unset.
.PP
On POSIX, a shell whose effective user is root neither loads nor writes
it.
.PP
\f[CR]koshconf create\f[R], \f[CR]koshconf set \-\-persist\f[R], and
\f[CR]koshconf save \-\-persist\f[R] write it.
.PP
A symbolic link is preserved and its target is written.
Existing permission bits are kept, and a new file receives mode 0644
less the creation mask.
Missing directories are created.
The previous file is copied to \f[I]kosh.conf.bak\f[R] beside it.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/kosh/scripts/rc.kosh or \[ti]/.config/kosh/scripts/rc.kosh\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The user rc script of a login or interactive \f[CR]kosh\f[R] mood is
described under Startup script.
.PP
The Bash and POSIX startup files that the other moods source are listed
in the shell reference.
.SH SETTINGS FILE SYNTAX
A settings file is a sequence of lines ended by LF or CRLF, and a
leading UTF\-8 byte order mark is skipped.
A line that is blank or starts with a number sign after optional blanks
is ignored.
Every other line has the form \f[I]name\f[R]=\f[I]value\f[R], with
blanks around the name and value ignored.
.PP
A value starting with a double or single quote ends at the next quote of
that kind, and only blanks may follow it, so a value whose surrounding
blanks must be kept needs quotes.
A value cannot span lines.
.IP \[bu] 2
A boolean is on or off, also accepted as true, false, 1, or 0.
.IP \[bu] 2
An enumeration is written as its value.
.IP \[bu] 2
A string must be valid UTF\-8 without a NUL byte.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[CR]editor.history.max_entries\f[R] is an integer from 0 to
2147483647.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[CR]editor.wrap_width\f[R] is an integer from 0 to 65535.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[CR]init_moods\f[R] is a comma\-separated list of moods without empty
elements.
.PP
A name may be the canonical name or its legacy alias.
.PP
A file may set every option except those fixed by how the shell started,
such as bash.interpreter.login_shell, and those that follow the session,
such as posix.interpreter.job_control, as listed under Shell settings.
A semantic option in a file changes every shell that loads it, scripts
included.
Unless every script should use such an option, it can be set for a
session with \f[CR]set\f[R], \f[CR]shopt\f[R], or
\f[CR]koshconf set\f[R].
.PP
The mood applies first, wherever it appears, and the other lines are
read under it.
In the \f[CR]kosh\f[R] mood, a line that sets another value for an
option the mood holds, as listed under Moods, is skipped with a warning.
.PP
A later line replaces an earlier one, and the user file and KOSHCONF
replace the system file.
.PP
Command\-line options and inherited KOSH_HISTORY_FILE or
KOSH_HISTORY_SIZE values take precedence over a settings file, as
described under Command\-line options and History.
.PP
\f[CR]koshconf\f[R] quotes a written value that starts with a quote or
starts or ends with a blank, and keeps the CRLF endings of a file that
uses them.
A value holding a newline, or one that needs quoting and holds both
quote characters, cannot be written, and
\f[CR]koshconf set \-\-persist\f[R] rejects it before changing the
session.
.PP
Persisting mood=kosh also removes the lines of the options the
\f[CR]kosh\f[R] mood fixes.
A line whose option this version does not recognize is kept at the end
of a rewritten file.
.PP
\f[CR]koshconf create\f[R] writes every option permitted in a settings
file except init_moods.
Each option has a help comment and its preset value.
Only the mood is active, and the remaining options are commented out,
allowing the system settings to apply until they are uncommented.
A string option without a default has an empty commented\-out value.
The \f[CR]kosh\f[R] preset uses the values fixed by that mood, so
uncommenting those options produces no warning.
.SH STARTUP SCRIPT
A login or interactive shell in the \f[CR]kosh\f[R] mood sources
scripts/rc.kosh in the user configuration directory after the settings
files.
The script can test \f[CR]shopt \-q login_shell\f[R] for login commands.
No system rc script, \[ti]/.koshrc, or /etc/koshrc is read.
The Startup files section describes when the script runs and the options
that replace or skip it.
Shell code belongs in the script, and options in \f[I]kosh.conf\f[R].
.PP
\f[CR]koshconf create\f[R] writes the following script when it is
missing and never replaces it.
.IP
.EX
\f[I]#!/usr/bin/env kosh\f[R]
\f[B]if\f[R] shopt \-q login_shell\f[B];\f[R] \f[B]then\f[R]
  [ \-r /etc/profile ] \f[B]&&\f[R] . /etc/profile
  \f[B]if\f[R] [ \-r \[ti]/.bash_profile ]\f[B];\f[R] \f[B]then\f[R]
    . \[ti]/.bash_profile
  \f[B]elif\f[R] [ \-r \[ti]/.bash_login ]\f[B];\f[R] \f[B]then\f[R]
    . \[ti]/.bash_login
  \f[B]elif\f[R] [ \-r \[ti]/.profile ]\f[B];\f[R] \f[B]then\f[R]
    . \[ti]/.profile
  \f[B]fi\f[R]
\f[B]else\f[R]
  [ \-r \[ti]/.profile ] \f[B]&&\f[R] . \[ti]/.profile
  [ \-r \[ti]/.bashrc ] \f[B]&&\f[R] . \[ti]/.bashrc
\f[B]fi\f[R]
.EE
.SH SHARING SETTINGS WITH KOSHCONF
\f[CR]KOSHCONF\f[R] is a dynamic shell variable holding the mood and the
interactive options in a binary form encoded as standard base64 with
padding.
.PP
It never carries the following options.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[CR]editor.history.file_path\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[CR]posix.interpreter.job_control\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[CR]bash.editor.history.bang_expansion\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[CR]bash.editor.history.records_commands\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[CR]bash.interpreter.login_shell\f[R]
.PP
An assignment to it is ignored.
.PP
It is not exported unless the user exports it, and after
\f[CR]export KOSHCONF\f[R], each started command receives the settings
current at its start.
A prefix assignment such as KOSHCONF=x before a command also passes the
current settings to that command.
.PP
A starting shell applies a KOSHCONF from its environment after the
settings files and removes it before any command runs, and
\f[CR]koshconf load\f[R] applies a value to a running shell.
The variable carries the settings to a remote shell in this way.
.IP
.EX
ssh host KOSHCONF=\[dq]$KOSHCONF\[dq] kosh
.EE
.SS Encoding format
The decoded bytes are a sequence of records, each an option id, a value
length, and the value.
The id and the length are unsigned LEB128 varints, with seven bits per
byte, the least significant group first, and the high bit set on every
byte but the last.
.IP \[bu] 2
A boolean is one byte, 0 or 1.
.IP \[bu] 2
An enumeration is a varint holding the position of the value in its
list.
.IP \[bu] 2
An integer is its decimal digits in ASCII.
.IP \[bu] 2
A string is its UTF\-8 bytes.
.PP
The ids are listed under Shell settings and never change meaning.
Semantic options never pass through the environment, and a child shell
loads the settings files itself.
.PP
A record with an unknown id, an option the encoding never carries, or a
value a settings file would reject is skipped with a warning.
Only the canonical form is accepted, so the base64 text carries its
padding, every varint uses the fewest bytes, and the ids strictly
ascend.
A value that breaks these rules, or has a truncated record, is rejected
as a whole with a warning.
.SH SETTINGS FILE ERRORS
A missing settings file is skipped.
.PP
An unreadable file, a file over 1 MiB, one that is not a regular file,
and a system file that fails the ownership check produce a warning and
are skipped.
.PP
A line without an equals sign, an unknown option, an invalid value, an
unclosed quote, text after a closing quote, and an option a file cannot
hold each produce a warning with the file and line, and the line is
skipped.
A settings file never stops the shell.
